/**
 * Skip Stop — why the express wins.
 *
 * A minimalist New York subway map, drawn from real track geometry, wrapped
 * around a small physics lesson: an express train has no higher top speed than
 * the local running beside it on the same trunk. It wins purely by not stopping.
 *
 * Every line, station and metre of track is the real thing. Track centrelines
 * and stopping patterns come from the MTA's GTFS feed — shapes.txt joined to the
 * *dominant* weekday trip pattern per route, not the longest, because the 4, A,
 * E and F all run local late at night and picking by length silently turns every
 * express into a local. PATH is folded in from the Port Authority feed, since it
 * is the one rapid-transit service here that actually crosses into New Jersey.
 * Land is NYC's shoreline-clipped borough boundaries plus US Census county
 * polygons for the surrounding region; the greens are NYC Parks properties.
 *
 * Where services share a trunk their colours coincide by design — 1/2/3 are all
 * red down Seventh Avenue — so overdraw is correct and the map stays clean.
 * Where differently-coloured services genuinely share track (2 beside 5 up White
 * Plains Road, B/D beside N/Q over the Manhattan Bridge) every vertex carries a
 * baked lane rank and is offset perpendicular at draw time, so they read as a
 * pair at any zoom instead of hiding one another.
 *
 * That same shared palette means a corridor's local and express are usually the
 * identical colour, so the round view carries its own visual grammar instead:
 * bold stroke and ringed stops are the express, hairline and small stops are the
 * local, and each train rides as its own route bullet.
 *
 * Each round frames one corridor — rotating the camera so the line runs up the
 * screen — states the kinematics, and asks for a prediction before dispatching.
 * Both trains then run the real geometry under a trapezoidal speed profile
 * (accelerate 1.0 m/s², cruise 72 km/h, brake 1.1 m/s², 30 s dwell) and the
 * result plots them as a distance–time graph, where the local's dwells are the
 * flat steps and the gap between the curves is the whole idea.
 *
 * A round is worth up to 1000 points, purely by how close the prediction landed
 * inside a tolerance that scales with the answer but is floored, so corridors
 * with small answers are not brutal. Five rounds go to the global board at the
 * end, which the finish screen renders in place — top five, your row picked out,
 * and pulled up from below if you placed outside it.
 *
 * One finger drags the map and two pinch it; a press only counts as a tap if it
 * barely moved, so panning never names a station by accident. And each train
 * rings as it pulls in — the local on C5, the express an octave above — so down
 * Lexington Avenue the local rings sixteen times to the express's four, which is
 * the whole lesson before the graph says it. Those cues are synthesised here
 * over WebAudio rather than drawn from ctx.music's stings: that engine is a
 * black box from inside a bit, every call wrapped and every failure silent, and
 * an unverifiable cue is worth nothing.
 */
